  "query_preview": "/FRIDAY THROUGH MONDAY/...\nIssued at 244 PM MDT Mon Aug 31 2026\n\nFriday through Monday, the upper level ridge continues to be \npersistent over the Southern United States with some amplification \ntowards the north over the weekend before stalling again. A strong \nlow pressure over the Pacific Northwest coast may allow some small \nembedded shortwave troughs to pass through as well as southwesterly \nflow from Baja California. This is forecast to bring in chances for \nshowers and warmer temperatures in the beginning of the period. \nDiurnal heating looks to be the main driving force for these \nshowers. Friday afternoon, Probability of Precipitation (PoPs) has \n15% chance for showers over the northwestern and 20% chance over the \neastern portions of the County Warning Area (CWA). There is high \nconfidence (70%) that it will be sub-severe as there are very low \nvalues of CAPE and an unfavorable wind shear profile. \n\nThroughout the remainder of the period, chances for rain will be low \nas the upper level ridge starts to amplify to the north. PoPs have a \nchance for showers at 15% in the northern half and 10% in southern \nhalf of the CWA. \n\nTemperatures will start out in the mid to upper 90s and are expected \nto decrease towards the low 90s by Monday. However, there is low \nconfidence (30%) that temperatures Sunday will be in the mid to low \n90s as NBM 75th to 25th percentile spread starts to increase with a \n5 degree difference and could potentially keep temperatures into the \nupper 90s. Uncertainty grows due to the possible showers later in \nthe weekend and Monday could lean to the cooler side. Heat index \nvalues may reach into the 100s on Sunday if highs reach into the \nupper 90s as moisture is being fed from both the southeast and \nsouthwest with dew points being in the low 60s.",
